{
 "_comment": "Files DELIBERATELY not pinned by tools/gate/vcpkg_cache_manifest.py, each with the measured reason. This list is EXPLICIT AND LITERAL -- no globs -- so coverage cannot be silently widened by a pattern; adding an entry requires a reviewed commit. Derived from a measured difference between two full from-source builds under x64-windows-repro, NOT from guesswork about what might vary.",
 "groups": {
  "build-metadata": "Generated build metadata that records paths/config of the build that produced it, not shipped program code.",
  "downstream-sbom": "vcpkg's generated SBOM records checksums OF THE BUILT FILES, so it differs as a consequence of the entries above rather than for a reason of its own. Its two per-document fields (created, documentNamespace) are normalised; these entries differ because the files they describe are unpinned.",
  "msvc-pdb": "MSVC PDB files are not reproducible: the format embeds build-specific layout and identifiers that /Brepro does not govern (it makes the IMAGE deterministic, not the symbol file). PDBs are never loaded by the running application, so an altered PDB cannot change program behaviour -- it can only mislead a debugger. Measured: 142 of them differed between two otherwise byte-identical from-source builds.",
  "self-timestamping-port": "The library embeds its OWN build timestamp in its output, above the toolchain layer, so no linker flag can remove it. Measured in libcrypto: the string 'built on: Tue Sep 15 10:26:06 2026 UTC' sits in the image and is the only meaningful residual after PE normalisation. Fixing this needs a per-port patch (the reproducible-builds treatment for OpenSSL/dbus/icu), which is real work and is NOT pretended to be done here.",
  "static-archive": "Static .lib archives are produced by lib.exe, which has no /Brepro equivalent; COFF archive members carry their own timestamps. Note these are the STATIC archives only -- import libraries for the deterministic DLLs verify normally and stay pinned.",
  "toolchain-abi-record": "vcpkg's own ABI bookkeeping for a port: it records the cmake, powershell and compiler identity (triplet_abi) plus portfile and patch hashes. Its CONTENT is the toolchain identity, which the manifest now records explicitly in its `toolchain` field, so pinning these files adds no integrity beyond that while guaranteeing 47 spurious CONTENTS CHANGED entries on every compiler or cmake update. Not shipped, not executed, and tampering with one cannot change what runs -- the binaries it describes stay pinned. Measured 2026-10-03: 47 of the 184 differences after the runner relocation were these files."
 },
